module Core

### Cycle-collector state for the Bacon-Rajan synchronous recycler (see
### internal-wiki/v0.4.x-cycle-collector.md). Touched only by the single-threaded collector.
choice RoamState
  ### In use — not garbage.
  LIVE: 0
  ### Being traced this cycle-collection pass.
  SCANNING: 1
  ### Garbage candidate (provisionally unreachable).
  GARBAGE: 2
  ### Buffered as a possible cycle root.
  SUSPECTED: 3

### Compiler intrinsic: yields a closure over this type's synthesized `roam_trace_impl` cycle-collector
### hook (visits the entity's `Roamed` fields). ExpressionLoweringPass rewrites every call to a
### routine-value reference to that hook; the `cptr_none()` body is a never-used fallback (NOT `@innate`
### — an unrecognized `@innate` T-method is folded to a default before the rewrite runs).
### See internal-wiki/v0.4.x-cycle-collector.md §9.3.
routine T.roam_trace_ref() -> CPtr
  return cptr_none()

### Compiler intrinsic: yields a closure over this type's synthesized `roam_free_impl` hook (tears down
### non-`Roamed` fields + frees the entity). Rewritten by ExpressionLoweringPass; `cptr_none()` fallback.
routine T.roam_free_ref() -> CPtr
  return cptr_none()

### Biased reference-counting controller for {Roamed[T]} — the hybrid of {RetainController[T]}
### (non-atomic, single-thread) and {GuardController[T, P]} (atomic + lock). Starts in LOCAL mode:
### one non-atomic `count`, no lock, owner only. On the first escape across a concurrency boundary
### `promote()` flips it — irreversibly — to ESCAPED mode: `count` becomes atomic and a reentrant,
### TASK-keyed exclusive lock is armed. The mode flag lives here, at the one address every {Roamed}
### handle shares, so all handles observe the flip.
###
### v1 biased scheme = SINGLE count + one-way flip (the classic dual-counter BRC, which keeps the
### owner fast even AFTER escape, is a deferred perf optimization). Reentrancy is keyed to the
### task/coroutine id (NOT the OS thread) because M:N migration moves a coroutine across workers.
### :typeparam T: The exclusively owned entity type.
entity RoamController[EntityType T]
  ### Reference count. Read/written non-atomically in LOCAL mode, atomically once ESCAPED.
  secret count: U64
  ### EXPERIMENT (object-level liveness): weak/observer count. A weak observer does NOT keep the entity
  ### alive; it keeps the CONTROLLER alive as a tombstone after the entity data is freed, so
  ### `is_destroyed()` can safely answer without dangling. LOCAL-only for now (non-atomic, single-thread);
  ### concurrency hardening (rwlock) is a follow-up.
  secret weak_count: U64
  ### EXPERIMENT (object-level liveness): set once the managed entity's DATA has been freed — via either
  ### free path (RC-drop `unhold` or cycle-collect `cyclic_finalize`). This is the path-agnostic ground
  ### truth for `is_destroyed()`: a CYCLE-collected node's `count` is NOT zero (internal cycle edges keep
  ### it up), so liveness cannot key on the count — it keys on this flag. The controller struct itself
  ### lives on as a tombstone while weak observers remain; only `dead` says the entity is gone.
  ### **`Atomic[U8]`, not `Bool`** (same reasoning as `escaped`): the free path publishes the flip and
  ### `is_destroyed()`/`is_alive()` read it from any thread — a plaintext `Bool` would be a data race and
  ### could be cached. `0` = live, `1` = dead. (`Bool` also lowers to `i1`, which has no HW atomic.)
  secret dead: Atomic[U8]
  ### `0` = LOCAL, `1` = ESCAPED. Flipped once by `promote()`. Every handle observes it.
  ### **`Atomic[U8]`, not `Bool`:** `promote()` publishes the flip cross-thread at the escape site
  ### and non-owner threads read it from `retain`/`release`/`guard_enter` — a plaintext `Bool` would
  ### be a data race (and could be hoisted/cached so a promoted object still looks LOCAL forever).
  ### SeqCst load/store on the atomic gives the release-store / acquire-load the flip needs. `Bool`
  ### also lowers to `i1`, which has no hardware atomic; the flag is `0`/`1` over `Atomic[U8]`.
  secret escaped: Atomic[U8]
  ### Raw pointer to the managed entity.
  secret data: Hijacked[T]
  ### Exclusive-lock flag (`0` free / `1` held). Armed and used only in ESCAPED mode.
  secret locked: Atomic[U8]
  ### Task/coroutine id of the current lock holder (`0` when free). Task-keyed for migration safety.
  secret holder_task: Atomic[U64]
  ### Reentrancy depth. Only ever touched by the holder under mutual exclusion, so non-atomic.
  secret depth: U64
  ### Cycle-collector state (Bacon-Rajan). Touched only by the single-threaded collector.
  secret state: RoamState
  ### `true` while this controller sits in the collector's roots buffer — dedups `mark_cycle_candidate`.
  secret buffered: Bool
  ### Address of the compiler-generated per-type trace function for `T` (visits `T`'s `Roamed`
  ### fields). `cptr_none()` = no trace wired yet / `T` has no `Roamed` fields (cannot form a cycle).
  ### Called as `fn(controller_address)`; reinterprets and `rf_cyclic_visit_child`s each `Roamed` field.
  secret trace_hook: CPtr
  ### Address of the compiler-generated per-type free function for `T`. Called as
  ### `fn(controller_address)` when the collector reaps a GARBAGE node: runs the managed
  ### entity's `destroy` (type-correct) then frees the controller. `cptr_none()` until wired.
  secret free_hook: CPtr

### Creates a controller with one strong reference in LOCAL mode (unlocked, non-atomic).
### :param data: The raw pointer to the entity; ownership transfers to this controller.
### :returns: The resulting `RoamController[T]` value.
dangerous routine RoamController[T](data: Hijacked[T]) -> RoamController[T]
  return RoamController[T](
    count: 1_u64,
    weak_count: 0_u64,
    dead: Atomic[U8](initial: 0_u8),
    escaped: Atomic[U8](initial: 0_u8),
    data: data,
    locked: Atomic[U8](initial: 0_u8),
    holder_task: Atomic[U64](initial: 0_u64),
    depth: 0_u64,
    state: RoamState.LIVE,
    buffered: false,
    trace_hook: data.as_entity().roam_trace_ref(),
    free_hook: data.as_entity().roam_free_ref()
  )

# ============================================================================
# Biased reference counting — branches on mode: non-atomic (LOCAL) vs atomic (ESCAPED).
# ============================================================================

### Adds an alias. Non-atomic in LOCAL mode (fast), atomic once ESCAPED. The count mutation is bracketed
### in the collector's SHARED stop-the-world lock so a concurrent cycle-collection pass (which trial-
### deletes counts non-atomically) can never race it. On the collector's own thread the lock is a no-op.
routine RoamController[T].hold()
  danger
    C::rf_cyclic_lock_shared()
    if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
      discard LLVM::atomic_add[U64](ptr: me.count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)
    else
      me.count = me.count + 1
    C::rf_cyclic_unlock_shared()
  return

### Drops an alias. At zero the managed entity is destroyed immediately (deterministic teardown).
### A decrement that does NOT reach zero registers the object as a cycle-collection candidate.
### The whole decrement — count drop, the buffered/eager-free branch, and the candidate report — runs
### under the collector's SHARED stop-the-world lock, so a collection pass can neither race the count nor
### free a controller it is mid-traversal over. `mark_cycle_candidate` and the eager free run WITHIN this
### bracket (no nested acquire). On the collector's own thread (a finalizer release) the lock is a no-op.
routine RoamController[T].unhold()
  danger
    C::rf_cyclic_lock_shared()
    if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
      var previous = LLVM::atomic_sub[U64](ptr: me.count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)
      if previous == 1_u64
        # Dead at zero — free eagerly (deterministic teardown). If it was a buffered cycle candidate,
        # first drop its roots entry so the collector never dereferences this freed controller.
        if me.buffered
          C::rf_cyclic_roots_remove(ptr: me.hijack().get_address())
        me.drop_data()
        me.dead.store(desired: 1_u8)
        # Keep the controller as a tombstone while weak observers remain, so `is_destroyed()` can safely
        # report the death (ESCAPED: atomic weak read). Freed only when weak also hits zero. This whole
        # branch runs under the SHARED lock, so it cannot race the collector's EXCLUSIVE pass.
        if LLVM::atomic_load[U64](ptr: me.weak_count.hijack()) == 0_u64
          me.destroy()
      else
        me.mark_cycle_candidate()
    else
      me.count = me.count - 1
      if me.count == 0_u64
        if me.buffered
          C::rf_cyclic_roots_remove(ptr: me.hijack().get_address())
        me.drop_data()
        me.dead.store(desired: 1_u8)
        # Tombstone while weak observers remain (LOCAL: plain weak read; see the ESCAPED branch above).
        if me.weak_count == 0_u64
          me.destroy()
      else
        me.mark_cycle_candidate()
    C::rf_cyclic_unlock_shared()
  return

### Reports this object to the cycle collector as a possible cycle root. The SOLE collector entry
### point — only `Roamed` decrements reach it, which is what scopes the collector to `Roamed`.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].mark_cycle_candidate()
  # Acyclic-type fast out (standard Bacon-Rajan optimization): a `T` with no `Roamed` fields cannot be
  # part of a cycle, and the compiler wires its `trace_hook` to `cptr_none()` (see `create`). Skipping
  # the candidate buffer here keeps the common non-zero decrement off the collector's books entirely.
  if me.trace_hook.is_none()
    return
  if me.buffered
    return
  me.buffered = true
  me.state = RoamState.SUSPECTED
  C::rf_cyclic_add_candidate(obj: me.hijack().get_address())
  # NO inline auto-collect: this runs under `unhold`'s SHARED stop-the-world lock, and a collection pass
  # takes the same lock EXCLUSIVE — triggering one here would deadlock (shared cannot upgrade to
  # exclusive). Collection is driven explicitly (`cyclic_collect()`) at a safe point; the threshold hook
  # (`rf_cyclic_should_collect`) is retained for a future safepoint-driven trigger outside any bracket.
  return

# ============================================================================
# Cycle-collector accessors. The collector (Core/Memory/CycleCollector.rf) holds each candidate as a
# type-erased `RoamController[RoamErased]` — every field below sits at a T-independent offset, so these
# read/write correctly through the erased view. Count adjustment is non-atomic: the collector runs
# single-threaded (core-local), so trial deletion touches the same count the RC uses without atomics.
# `cyclic_set_hooks` is a test/wiring hatch (compiler auto-wires trace_hook/free_hook in Stage 2).
# ============================================================================

### The address of the managed entity — the collector passes this to the entity-based trace/free
### hooks (`roam_trace_impl` / `roam_free_impl`, whose `me` is the entity). :returns: the entity address.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_data_addr() -> Address
  danger
    return me.data.get_address()

### The collector state. :returns: this controller's `RoamState`.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_state() -> RoamState
  return me.state

### Sets the collector state. :param c: the new state.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_set_state(c: RoamState)
  me.state = c
  return

### Whether this controller is buffered as a root. :returns: the `buffered` flag.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_buffered() -> Bool
  return me.buffered

### Sets the buffered flag. :param b: the new value.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_set_buffered(b: Bool)
  me.buffered = b
  return

### Non-atomic strong count. :returns: the current count.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_count() -> U64
  return me.count

### Trial-deletion decrement (non-atomic).
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_dec()
  me.count = me.count - 1
  return

### Trial-deletion restore increment (non-atomic).
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_inc()
  me.count = me.count + 1
  return

### The per-type trace function address. :returns: `trace_hook`.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_trace_hook() -> CPtr
  return me.trace_hook

### The per-type free function address. :returns: `free_hook`.
routine RoamController[T].cyclic_free_hook() -> CPtr
  return me.free_hook

### Wires the per-type trace/free hooks (test/Stage-1 hatch). :param trace: trace fn. :param free: free fn.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_set_hooks(trace: CPtr, free: CPtr)
  me.trace_hook = trace
  me.free_hook = free
  return

### Returns the current strong count. Snapshot only.
### :returns: The number of live strong handles.
routine RoamController[T].strong_count() -> U64
  danger
    if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
      return LLVM::atomic_load[U64](ptr: me.count.hijack())
    else
      return me.count

# ============================================================================
# EXPERIMENT — object-level liveness (weak observation + tombstone). LOCAL/single-thread only.
# A weak observer keeps the CONTROLLER alive after the entity data is freed, so `is_destroyed()`
# can safely answer without dangling. `observe`/`unobserve` mirror `hold`/`unhold` for the weak count.
# ============================================================================

### Registers a weak observer (does NOT keep the entity alive; keeps the controller as a tombstone).
### Bracketed in the collector's SHARED lock and atomic once ESCAPED — mirrors `hold`, so a concurrent
### collection pass (EXCLUSIVE) can never race the weak count or free a controller mid-`cyclic_finalize`.
### SAFE PRECONDITION: only ever called through an already-kept reference (a live strong handle or another
### weak observer), so the controller is guaranteed alive at entry — no observe-races-free window.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].observe()
  C::rf_cyclic_lock_shared()
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    discard LLVM::atomic_add[U64](ptr: me.weak_count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)
  else
    me.weak_count = me.weak_count + 1
  C::rf_cyclic_unlock_shared()
  return

### Drops a weak observer. When the LAST observer leaves a DEAD (tombstoned) controller, the controller
### struct is finally freed. If the entity is still alive, only the observer count drops. Bracketed +
### atomic like `unhold` (last-drop detected by the atomic-sub's previous value in ESCAPED mode).
dangerous routine RoamController[T].unobserve()
  C::rf_cyclic_lock_shared()
  var last = false
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    var previous = LLVM::atomic_sub[U64](ptr: me.weak_count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)
    if previous == 1_u64
      last = true
  else
    me.weak_count = me.weak_count - 1
    if me.weak_count == 0_u64
      last = true
  if last and me.dead.load() == 1_u8
    me.destroy()
  C::rf_cyclic_unlock_shared()
  return

### Whether the observed entity is still alive. Safe on a tombstone (reads the atomic `dead` flag, never
### `data`). A lock-free snapshot, like `strong_count`. :returns: `true` until the entity's data is freed.
routine RoamController[T].is_alive() -> Bool
  danger
    return me.dead.load() == 0_u8

### Whether the observed entity has been destroyed. Path-agnostic: true once the entity DATA is freed by
### the RC-drop (`unhold`) OR the cycle-collect (`cyclic_finalize`) path — NOT keyed on the strong count,
### which a cycle-collected node keeps non-zero via its internal edges. :returns: `true` once it is gone.
routine RoamController[T].is_destroyed() -> Bool
  danger
    return me.dead.load() == 1_u8

### Cycle-collect finalizer counterpart to the RC-drop tombstone in `unhold`: the collector calls this
### AFTER the free hook has torn down the entity data. Marks the controller dead and frees the controller
### struct only when no weak observer remains — otherwise it lives on as a tombstone so `is_destroyed()`
### stays answerable. Runs UNDER the collector's EXCLUSIVE lock (the finalizer path), so it takes NO
### shared lock (that would self-deadlock, per the §2 `rf_in_collector` rule) and the weak read needs no
### bracket — no mutator runs concurrently. :remarks: T-independent fields only → sound via the erased view.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].cyclic_finalize()
  me.dead.store(desired: 1_u8)
  var w = 0_u64
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    w = LLVM::atomic_load[U64](ptr: me.weak_count.hijack())
  else
    w = me.weak_count
  if w == 0_u64
    me.destroy()
  return

# ============================================================================
# Escape — one-way LOCAL -> ESCAPED promotion.
# ============================================================================

### Flips to ESCAPED (idempotent). Runs on the owner BEFORE the handle is published to another
### thread (the compiler inserts it at the escape site, pre-spawn), so no concurrent access races
### the flip. After this, `count` is atomic and the reentrant lock is live.
### Escape is a GRAPH property, not per-object: everything reachable through this object's lock is
### reachable by the crossing task too, so it must escape as well (a one-object flip would leave a LOCAL
### child — non-atomic count, no lock — that both the owner and the crossing task then touch, a data race).
### Transitive escape walks the `Roamed` children via the per-type trace hook. ITERATIVE (explicit worklist,
### NOT recursion) — a deep Roamed graph would otherwise overflow the call stack. The escaped-check prunes
### already-escaped nodes, so cyclic graphs terminate and repeated promotion is amortized. Children are
### viewed through `RoamController[T]` purely for the fields touched here (`escaped`/`holder_task`/`depth`/
### `trace_hook`/`data`, all at a T-INDEPENDENT offset) — reusing `T` on a differently-typed child is a
### sound reinterpretation. A type with no `Roamed` fields (`trace_hook` = `cptr_none()`) owns no edge.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].promote()
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    return
  var work = List[Address]()
  work.add_last(value: me.hijack().get_address())
  while not work.is_empty()
    var addr = work.remove_last!()
    var node = Hijacked[RoamController[T]](addr).as_entity()
    if node.escaped.load() == 0_u8
      node.holder_task.store(desired: 0_u64)
      node.depth = 0_u64
      node.escaped.store(desired: 1_u8)
      # Trace THIS node's children into the shared native scratch, then drain immediately into the
      # worklist before the next iteration's trace clobbers it (same discipline as `cyclic_children`).
      if not node.trace_hook.is_none()
        C::rf_cyclic_scratch_reset()
        C::rf_cyclic_trace_into_scratch(trace_hook: node.trace_hook.get_address(), controller: node.data.get_address())
        var n = C::rf_cyclic_scratch_count()
        var i = 0_u64
        while i < n
          work.add_last(value: C::rf_cyclic_scratch_at(i: i))
          i = i + 1_u64
  return

### Whether this object has escaped. Drives `Roamed`'s access-lock wrapping.
### :returns: `true` if ESCAPED.
routine RoamController[T].is_escaped() -> Bool
  return me.escaped.load() == 1_u8

# ============================================================================
# Reentrant, task-keyed exclusive lock — active only in ESCAPED mode.
# ============================================================================

### Acquires the exclusive lock, spinning while another task holds it. If the CALLING task already
### holds it, this is a re-entry: the depth is bumped and it returns without re-acquiring — so a
### member routine calling itself (normal class recursion) never self-deadlocks. Keyed to the
### task/coroutine id, not the OS thread, so a migrated coroutine still recognizes its own hold.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].reentrant_acquire()
  var tid = C::rf_current_task_id()
  loop
    if me.holder_task.load() == tid and me.locked.load() == 1_u8
      me.depth = me.depth + 1
      break
    if me.locked.compare_exchange(expected: 0_u8, desired: 1_u8)
      me.holder_task.store(desired: tid)
      me.depth = 1_u64
      break
    # Contended by another task. Register the wait (waiting for the current holder) so the opt-in §4
    # deadlock detector can spot an opposite-order lock cycle and abort LOUDLY rather than hang; a
    # no-op unless RF_DEADLOCK_DETECT is set. Then yield instead of a bare spin: under M:N the holder
    # may be a coroutine parked on this very worker (or awaiting reschedule after migration), and a
    # tight spin would pin the worker and livelock when workers are scarce. `rf_coro_yield` re-queues
    # this coroutine so the holder can run; off a coroutine (top-level thread) it is a no-op busy-spin.
    C::rf_deadlock_wait_begin(self: tid, holder: me.holder_task.load())
    C::rf_coro_yield()
  # Acquired (fresh or re-entrant): clear any wait we registered while contended.
  C::rf_deadlock_wait_end(self: tid)
  return

### Releases one level of the lock. The lock is freed only when the outermost holder releases (depth
### reaches zero); the holder is cleared before the flag so a waiter never observes the old id.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].reentrant_release()
  me.depth = me.depth - 1
  if me.depth == 0_u64
    me.holder_task.store(desired: 0_u64)
    me.locked.store(desired: 0_u8)
  return

### Mode-checked lock entry: acquires the reentrant lock only when ESCAPED (a no-op in LOCAL mode,
### where there is no contention). Drives `Roamed`'s access wrapping and the `Roaming[T]` guard token.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].guard_enter()
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    me.reentrant_acquire()
  return

### Mode-checked lock exit: the `guard_enter` counterpart.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].guard_exit()
  if me.escaped.load() == 1_u8
    me.reentrant_release()
  return

# ============================================================================
# Teardown.
# ============================================================================

### Returns a non-owning pointer to the managed entity.
### :returns: The resulting `Hijacked[T]` value.
routine RoamController[T].data_ptr() -> Hijacked[T]
  return me.data

### Destroys the managed entity. Call only when the strong count reaches zero.
dangerous routine RoamController[T].drop_data()
  me.data.as_entity().destroy()
  return

@override
dangerous routine RoamController[T].destroy()
  me.hijack().invalidate()
  return
